    for (size_t i = 0; i < bytes_read; i++) {
        checksum += buffer[i];
    }

    // Save number of bytes read and checksum to another file
    FILE *output_file = fopen("output.txt", "w");
    if (output_file == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening output file\n");
        free(buffer);
        fclose(file);
        return 1;
    }
    fprintf(output_file, "%zu %d\n", bytes_read, checksum);
    fclose(output_file);

    // Free memory allocated for buffer
    free(buffer);
    fclose(file);

    return 0;
}
```
This code reads the contents of a file into a buffer using `fread()`, calculates the checksum of the buffer using a loop, and saves the number of bytes read and checksum to another file. The `BUFFER_SIZE` macro is used to specify the size of the buffer in terms of bytes.

Note that this code assumes that the input file is a text file, and that the output file should also be a text file. If the input or output files are binary files, then you may need to use a different function such as `fread()` with the `binary` flag set to `1`.']

