Here is a possible implementation of the `http_hello_page` function in C:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

void http_hello_page(FILE* request, FILE* response) {
    char username[1024];
    fread(username, 1024, 1, request);
    username[strlen(username) - 1] = '\0'; // remove newline character at end of string

    char* escaped_username = escape_html(username);
    char response_buffer[1024];
    sprintf(response_buffer, "hello %s", escaped_username);
    fwrite(response_buffer, strlen(response_buffer), 1, response);

    free(escaped_username);
}

char* escape_html(const char* input) {
    // escape special HTML characters such as <, >, and &
    const char* special_chars = "<>&";
    size_t length = strlen(input);
    for (size_t i = 0; i < length; i++) {
        if (strchr(special_chars, input[i])) {
            input[i] = ' '; // replace special characters with spaces
        }
    }

    return input;
}
```
This implementation reads the username from the request file stream using `fread`, and then removes the newline character at the end of the string using `strlen` and `char*`. The function then calls the `escape_html` function to escape any special HTML characters in the username, and writes the escaped username to the response file stream using `sprintf` and `fwrite`. Finally, the function frees the memory allocated for the escaped username using `free`.

Note that this implementation assumes that the request file stream contains a valid username string, and that the response file stream is open for writing. If either of these assumptions is not met, the behavior of the function is undefined.']

